<wpf:ResourceDictionary xml:space="preserve" xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml" xmlns:s="clr-namespace:System;assembly=mscorlib" xmlns:ss="urn:shemas-jetbrains-com:settings-storage-xaml" xmlns:wpf="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation">
	<!--
	    SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only

	    Team-shared ReSharper/Rider settings. This layer sits ABOVE "This computer" (personal global
	    settings) and BELOW "Solution personal" (DwarfMapper.NET.sln.DotSettings.user), so it is the right
	    place for conventions that belong to the REPOSITORY rather than to a machine. It has to be COMMITTED
	    to be shared; an uncommitted file only helps the machine it sits on.

	    It carries NAMING POLICY ONLY. Nothing here is read by MSBuild, Roslyn or the analyzer packages, so
	    nothing here can change the build or the tests. Narrowly-scoped inspection severities live in
	    .editorconfig instead, because a DotSettings severity is solution-wide and cannot be limited to a
	    directory.

	    Every entry below states an ACCEPTED PATTERN. None of them switches an inspection off.

	    Two of the three rules override rules that already exist in the "This computer" layer and are
	    addressed by their GUID; a clone that does not have those personal rules simply gains a rule that
	    permits what the repository already writes, which is harmless.
	-->
	<!--
	    PUBLIC CLASSES (and record classes) - UpperCamelCase, or Corpus_UpperCamelCase.
	    The test corpora encode WHICH corpus a fixture belongs to in an underscore-separated prefix:
	    Cc_ (computed-null), Ce_ (enum/collection), Cf_ (flatten/rename), Ec_ (edge-case parity),
	    Par_ (library parity), Nf_ (new features), Rf_, Sec_. 244 public classes carry one. The prefix is
	    the corpus index - it is what makes a fixture greppable back to the corpus that owns it - so
	    flattening Cc_Person to CcPerson would delete the only information the prefix carries.
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeStyle/Naming/CSharpNaming/UserRules/=26f6df82_002D24db_002D4a53_002Daec3_002D2895ec20c0c1/@EntryIndexedValue">&lt;Policy&gt;&lt;Descriptor Staticness="Static, Instance" AccessRightKinds="Public" Description="Public classes: UpperCamelCase, or Corpus_UpperCamelCase for the prefixed test fixtures"&gt;&lt;ElementKinds&gt;&lt;Kind Name="CLASS" /&gt;&lt;/ElementKinds&gt;&lt;/Descriptor&gt;&lt;Policy Inspect="True" Prefix="" Suffix="" Style="AaBb"&gt;&lt;ExtraRule Prefix="" Suffix="" Style="AaBb_AaBb" /&gt;&lt;/Policy&gt;&lt;/Policy&gt;</s:String>
	<!--
	    LOCAL CONSTANTS - lowerCamelCase, or UpperCamelCase.
	    The generator and compiler test suites hold the C# source under test in a local constant
	    conventionally named `src`, `s`, `source` or `code` - 836 of them across 139 files. The name is the
	    subject-matter noun: the test reads "compile THIS source". The rule this replaces (same GUID, in the
	    personal layer) demanded a leading underscore and lumped CLASS in with LOCAL_CONSTANT, which is why
	    `const string source` was reported with the suggestion `_source`; an underscore prefix is a
	    private-field convention and was never a local-constant or class convention here. Narrowed to local
	    constants, underscore dropped, UpperCamelCase kept for the constants that spell out a value.
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeStyle/Naming/CSharpNaming/UserRules/=466cff5f_002Daaec_002D4d64_002D9a77_002Dfe69d32ccfa5/@EntryIndexedValue">&lt;Policy&gt;&lt;Descriptor Staticness="Static, Instance" AccessRightKinds="Private, Public" Description="Local constants: lowerCamelCase (src/s/source/code hold the source under test), or UpperCamelCase"&gt;&lt;ElementKinds&gt;&lt;Kind Name="LOCAL_CONSTANT" /&gt;&lt;/ElementKinds&gt;&lt;/Descriptor&gt;&lt;Policy Inspect="True" Prefix="" Suffix="" Style="aaBb"&gt;&lt;ExtraRule Prefix="" Suffix="" Style="AaBb" /&gt;&lt;/Policy&gt;&lt;/Policy&gt;</s:String>
	<!--
	    ENUMS AND STRUCTS - same corpus-prefix convention, same policy. The rule above covers classes and
	    record classes only; the six enums and two structs that carry a corpus prefix (Ce_Priority,
	    Ce_ColorA, ...) would otherwise still be reported by the built-in "Types and namespaces" rule.
	    Interfaces are deliberately NOT listed: the `I` prefix they are held to is a convention this
	    repository keeps, and no interface here carries a corpus prefix.
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeStyle/Naming/CSharpNaming/UserRules/=d1c6b3a2_002D4f7e_002D4a90_002D9b21_002D3e8c5f0a7d64/@EntryIndexedValue">&lt;Policy&gt;&lt;Descriptor Staticness="Static, Instance" AccessRightKinds="Private, Public" Description="Enums and structs: UpperCamelCase, or Corpus_UpperCamelCase for the prefixed test fixtures"&gt;&lt;ElementKinds&gt;&lt;Kind Name="ENUM" /&gt;&lt;Kind Name="STRUCT" /&gt;&lt;/ElementKinds&gt;&lt;/Descriptor&gt;&lt;Policy Inspect="True" Prefix="" Suffix="" Style="AaBb"&gt;&lt;ExtraRule Prefix="" Suffix="" Style="AaBb_AaBb" /&gt;&lt;/Policy&gt;&lt;/Policy&gt;</s:String>
</wpf:ResourceDictionary>
